    {
      "id": "adr-trunk",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Trunk-based development",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "code-review-branching",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Several people change the code of this element every day, and every change travels from one machine to the main branch through a branch, a review and a merge. The forces: a branch that lives long drifts from main and merges with pain, but one merged within hours needs a way to hide unfinished work; a review catches the mistakes tests do not describe, but a review that waits a day stalls every change behind it and a review of 2,000 lines catches almost nothing (SmartBear: defect density falls off past about 400 lines); the people who know a directory best are not always free to review it; the history of main is what the next incident is debugged from, so what a commit message says and whether history is linear matters a year later; and products shipped in numbered versions to customers who install them need branches for versions in support, which a service deployed continuously never does. The snippets assume GitHub (rulesets, the GitHub CLI and GraphQL API, GitHub Actions) and one repository layout with source in src/ and the workflow files under .github/workflows/.",
      "decision": "The element is developed trunk-based on GitHub: a change is a branch of at most {{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}} days merged to main through a short pull request, and unfinished work ships behind a feature flag (OpenFeature with the provider the Deployment decision names) or a branch by abstraction. Main is protected by a ruleset: no direct push or force push, required checks (the Code quality gate and Test strategy jobs), linear history by squash merge. Review: {{REVIEW}}. Ownership: {{OWNERSHIP}}. Size: {{SIZE}}. Commits: {{COMMITS}}. A first review is due within {{REVIEW_HOURS}} working hours; a daily job lists branches older than {{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}} days.",
      "consequences": "• Merges are small and conflicts rare, because nobody is more than two days away from main; lead time for a change is hours.\n• Unfinished work needs flags or abstraction seams, and flags need clean-up (the Deployment pack's stale-flag check).\n• Every developer must split work into steps that each leave main releasable, which is a skill the team has to build.\n• There is no branch to stabilise a release on: the test suite and the rollout checks are the only safety net.",
      "alternatives": "1. Feature branches with a pull request each: no flags needed for unfinished work, but branches live longer and merges hurt more.\n2. GitFlow: a branch per release, right for installed products, needless ceremony for a service deployed continuously.\n3. Direct commits to main without pull requests: faster still, but the review and the checks have nowhere to run before the code lands.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "trunk"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-feature-pr",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Feature branches with pull requests",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "code-review-branching",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Several people change the code of this element every day, and every change travels from one machine to the main branch through a branch, a review and a merge. The forces: a branch that lives long drifts from main and merges with pain, but one merged within hours needs a way to hide unfinished work; a review catches the mistakes tests do not describe, but a review that waits a day stalls every change behind it and a review of 2,000 lines catches almost nothing (SmartBear: defect density falls off past about 400 lines); the people who know a directory best are not always free to review it; the history of main is what the next incident is debugged from, so what a commit message says and whether history is linear matters a year later; and products shipped in numbered versions to customers who install them need branches for versions in support, which a service deployed continuously never does. The snippets assume GitHub (rulesets, the GitHub CLI and GraphQL API, GitHub Actions) and one repository layout with source in src/ and the workflow files under .github/workflows/.",
      "decision": "Every change to the element is a branch from main and a pull request on GitHub, rebased on main before merge and merged by squash when reviewed and green; main is always releasable and nothing reaches it any other way. Branches stay under {{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}} days, and a daily job flags the ones that do not. Main is protected by a ruleset: no direct push or force push, required checks (the Code quality gate and Test strategy jobs), linear history. Review: {{REVIEW}}. Ownership: {{OWNERSHIP}}. Size: {{SIZE}}. Commits: {{COMMITS}}. A first review is due within {{REVIEW_HOURS}} working hours.",
      "consequences": "• Every change gets its review and its checks before it lands; main is releasable at any commit.\n• Unfinished work can stay on its branch, so no feature flags are needed for it, but a branch past a week drifts and merges with pain: the age check is what keeps this model honest.\n• Two branches that touch the same files find out at merge time, later than trunk-based development would.\n• Pull requests are the unit of history and discussion; the squash commit links to it.",
      "alternatives": "1. Trunk-based development: shorter lead time and fewer merge conflicts, at the price of feature flags and smaller steps.\n2. GitFlow: adds develop, release and hotfix branches that a continuously deployed service does not need.\n3. Long-lived feature branches merged when the feature is done: the merges become projects of their own and main stops reflecting the team's work.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "feature-pr"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-gitflow",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "GitFlow with release and hotfix branches",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "code-review-branching",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Several people change the code of this element every day, and every change travels from one machine to the main branch through a branch, a review and a merge. The forces: a branch that lives long drifts from main and merges with pain, but one merged within hours needs a way to hide unfinished work; a review catches the mistakes tests do not describe, but a review that waits a day stalls every change behind it and a review of 2,000 lines catches almost nothing (SmartBear: defect density falls off past about 400 lines); the people who know a directory best are not always free to review it; the history of main is what the next incident is debugged from, so what a commit message says and whether history is linear matters a year later; and products shipped in numbered versions to customers who install them need branches for versions in support, which a service deployed continuously never does. The snippets assume GitHub (rulesets, the GitHub CLI and GraphQL API, GitHub Actions) and one repository layout with source in src/ and the workflow files under .github/workflows/. Here the element ships in numbered versions that customers install and keep running, so a version in support needs a branch of its own to be fixed on.",
      "decision": "The element follows GitFlow on GitHub: feature branches merge to develop through pull requests; a release/x.y branch is cut from develop for each version, takes fixes only, and is merged to main with a merge commit and an annotated tag vX.Y.Z and back into develop; a hotfix/x.y.z branch is cut from the tag on main and merged to both. Main and develop are protected by rulesets (no direct or force push, a pull request with an approval, required checks from the Code quality gate and Test strategy decisions); main accepts merges from release/* and hotfix/* only, so its history holds merge commits and is not linear. Review: {{REVIEW}}. Ownership: {{OWNERSHIP}}. Size: {{SIZE}}. Commits: {{COMMITS}}. Feature branches stay under {{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}} days; a first review is due within {{REVIEW_HOURS}} working hours. The git-flow AVH command-line extension does the branching; a GitHub Actions job checks main's history and develop's protection.",
      "consequences": "• Each supported version has a branch to fix on; customers on 2.3 get 2.3.1 without 2.4's features.\n• Two long-lived branches (main, develop) and a release branch per version mean double merges and the occasional fix forgotten on one side; the back-merge is checked, not trusted.\n• Integration happens on develop, later than on a trunk; the age limit on feature branches keeps it from drifting further.\n• The model's own author advises against it for software deployed continuously; if the element ever becomes a service, this decision is superseded by trunk-based development.",
      "alternatives": "1. Trunk-based development with release branches cut from main and fixes cherry-picked back: fewer long-lived branches, enough for most versioned products (Hammant: branch for release).\n2. Feature branches with pull requests and tags on main only: no place to fix a version in support once main has moved on.\n3. One branch per customer version kept forever: unbounded maintenance.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "gitflow"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-codeowners",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Code owners per directory",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "code-review-branching",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Several people change the code of this element every day, and every change travels from one machine to the main branch through a branch, a review and a merge. The forces: a branch that lives long drifts from main and merges with pain, but one merged within hours needs a way to hide unfinished work; a review catches the mistakes tests do not describe, but a review that waits a day stalls every change behind it and a review of 2,000 lines catches almost nothing (SmartBear: defect density falls off past about 400 lines); the people who know a directory best are not always free to review it; the history of main is what the next incident is debugged from, so what a commit message says and whether history is linear matters a year later; and products shipped in numbered versions to customers who install them need branches for versions in support, which a service deployed continuously never does. The snippets assume GitHub (rulesets, the GitHub CLI and GraphQL API, GitHub Actions) and one repository layout with source in src/ and the workflow files under .github/workflows/.",
      "decision": "A CODEOWNERS file at .github/CODEOWNERS maps every top-level directory of the element to a GitHub team; a catch-all line at the top names the platform team for anything unlisted. The ruleset requires a code-owner review, so a change under a directory waits for its team, which GitHub requests automatically. Critical directories ({{CRITICAL_PATHS}}) are owned by the team accountable for them. A CI check parses CODEOWNERS and fails when a top-level directory has no owner or an owner is not a team, and reads the ruleset to confirm the code-owner review is required.",
      "consequences": "• The people who know a directory see every change to it, and a change in payments/ cannot be waved through by a stranger.\n• A team that is slow to review becomes a bottleneck for everyone touching its directory; the turnaround measure shows where.\n• The file must be kept in step with the directory tree; the check fails the build when it is not.\n• Cross-cutting changes collect several owners and several approvals.",
      "alternatives": "1. Collective ownership with rotating reviewers: no bottleneck per directory, but nobody accountable for one either.\n2. Owners listed in a wiki: not enforced, out of date within a month.\n3. One owner for the whole repository: a single person on every pull request's path.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "OWNERSHIP": [
          "codeowners"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-collective",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Collective ownership with rotating reviewers",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "code-review-branching",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Several people change the code of this element every day, and every change travels from one machine to the main branch through a branch, a review and a merge. The forces: a branch that lives long drifts from main and merges with pain, but one merged within hours needs a way to hide unfinished work; a review catches the mistakes tests do not describe, but a review that waits a day stalls every change behind it and a review of 2,000 lines catches almost nothing (SmartBear: defect density falls off past about 400 lines); the people who know a directory best are not always free to review it; the history of main is what the next incident is debugged from, so what a commit message says and whether history is linear matters a year later; and products shipped in numbered versions to customers who install them need branches for versions in support, which a service deployed continuously never does. The snippets assume GitHub (rulesets, the GitHub CLI and GraphQL API, GitHub Actions) and one repository layout with source in src/ and the workflow files under .github/workflows/.",
      "decision": "Nobody owns a directory: anyone on the team may change and review any part of the element, and there is no CODEOWNERS file. The team's review assignment on GitHub is set to round robin with one reviewer per pull request, so a review request lands within minutes of ready-for-review and reviews spread across the team; a weekly job reads the reviews of the last 30 days and fails when one person gave more than 40% of them or a request took longer than 15 minutes. Other packs that need an owner of a directory take the whole team.",
      "consequences": "• No bottleneck on one team or person; knowledge of every part spreads with the rotation.\n• Nobody is accountable for a directory, so a critical directory relies on the review count, not on who reviews it.\n• Works for one team of up to about eight; beyond that the rotation lands reviews on people who do not know the code.\n• The assignment setting lives in the team's configuration on GitHub and is checked, not trusted.",
      "alternatives": "1. CODEOWNERS per directory: accountable owners and automatic requests, with a bottleneck per team.\n2. Volunteers picking reviews from a queue: reviews go to the same two people within a month.\n3. Load-balanced assignment: fewer reviews for the busy, but reviews stop spreading evenly.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "OWNERSHIP": [
          "collective"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-branch-protection",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Main is protected by a ruleset",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "structural",
      "description": "A scheduled job reads the rules in force on main through the GitHub rulesets API and fails when one of the required rules is missing or weaker than the decision, so a setting changed by hand in the web UI is noticed within a day. The endpoint returns ruleset rules only: a repository still on classic branch protection moves to a ruleset first.\n\nThreshold: The rules active on main include pull_request with at least 1 required approval and stale-review dismissal, required_status_checks with strict mode and at least one context, non_fast_forward (no force push) and deletion; 0 of them missing\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# daily: fails when a rule required by the branching decision is missing on main (rulesets only)\nset -euo pipefail\nrules=$(gh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/rules/branches/main\")\nneed() { echo \"$rules\" | jq -e \"$1\" > /dev/null || { echo \"missing on main: $2\"; exit 1; }; }\nneed 'map(select(.type==\"pull_request\" and .parameters.required_approving_review_count>=1 and .parameters.dismiss_stale_reviews_on_push)) | length>0' 'pull request with 1 approval, stale reviews dismissed'\nneed 'map(select(.type==\"required_status_checks\" and .parameters.strict_required_status_checks_policy and (.parameters.required_status_checks|length>0))) | length>0' 'required status checks (strict, at least one)'\nneed 'map(select(.type==\"non_fast_forward\")) | length>0' 'no force push'\nneed 'map(select(.type==\"deletion\")) | length>0' 'no deletion'\necho \"main is protected as decided\"\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-linear-history",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Main history is linear and reviewed",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "description": "A weekly job reads the rules on main and the repository's merge settings through the GitHub API and fails unless linear history is required and merge commits are off; then it walks the commits on main of the last 30 days and asks the API which pull request each belongs to, failing on a commit that belongs to none.\n\nThreshold: The rules active on main include required_linear_history; merge commits are disabled in the repository; 0 commits on main in the last 30 days without an associated merged pull request\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api) with git in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# weekly: linear history required, merge commits off, every commit on main from a pull request\nset -euo pipefail\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/rules/branches/main\" | jq -e 'map(select(.type==\"required_linear_history\")) | length>0' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"::error::main does not require linear history\"; exit 1; }\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY\" | jq -e '.allow_merge_commit | not' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"::error::merge commits are allowed in the repository settings\"; exit 1; }\ngit fetch --no-tags origin main\nbad=0\nfor sha in $(git log --since='30 days ago' --format=%H origin/main); do\n  n=$(gh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/commits/$sha/pulls\" --jq 'map(select(.merged_at != null)) | length')\n  [ \"$n\" -ge 1 ] || { echo \"::error::$sha reached main without a merged pull request\"; bad=1; }\ndone\nexit $bad\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "trunk",
          "feature-pr"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-codeowners",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every directory has an owner",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "structural",
      "description": "The CODEOWNERS file names a team per top-level directory with a catch-all first (* @shop/platform, then payments/ @shop/payments, auth/ @shop/identity, migrations/ @shop/data, web/ @shop/storefront); a CI step walks the top-level directories and fails when one has no line of its own, or when an owner is a single person instead of a team, and reads the rules on main to confirm a code-owner review is required.\n\nThreshold: 0 top-level directories without a matching CODEOWNERS line; every owner is a team (@org/team); the rules on main include pull_request with require_code_owner_review\n\nTool: GitHub CODEOWNERS with a shell check and gh api in CI\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# CI step: every top-level directory owned by a team, and the ruleset asks the owners\nset -euo pipefail\nfor dir in $(git ls-tree -d --name-only HEAD | grep -v '^\\.'); do\n  grep -Eq \"^$dir/\\s+@[a-z0-9-]+/[a-z0-9-]+\" .github/CODEOWNERS || { echo \"no team owns $dir/\"; exit 1; }\ndone\nif grep -Eq '^\\S+\\s+@[a-z0-9-]+\\s*$' .github/CODEOWNERS; then echo \"an owner is a person, not a team\"; exit 1; fi\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/rules/branches/main\" | jq -e 'map(select(.type==\"pull_request\" and .parameters.require_code_owner_review)) | length>0' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"the ruleset on main does not require a code-owner review\"; exit 1; }\necho \"every top-level directory has an owning team, and the ruleset asks it\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "OWNERSHIP": [
          "codeowners"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-review-rotation",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Reviews rotate and arrive in minutes",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "holistic",
      "description": "A weekly script reads the team's review-assignment settings and the last 100 merged pull requests through the GraphQL API, keeps the ones merged in the last 30 days, measures the minutes from ready-for-review (or creation) to the first review request, counts the reviews per reviewer and fails on a request slower than 15 minutes, a reviewer above 40%, or an assignment algorithm other than round robin.\n\nThreshold: The team's review assignment is round robin; over the pull requests merged in the last 30 days, every review request came within 15 minutes of ready-for-review and no reviewer gave more than 40% of the reviews\n\nTool: GitHub GraphQL API (gh api graphql) with a Node script\n\n```ts\n// scripts/review-rotation.ts  (weekly; TEAM=<team slug>)\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nconst [owner, name] = process.env.GITHUB_REPOSITORY!.split('/')\nconst gql = (query: string, vars: Record<string, string>) =>\n  JSON.parse(execFileSync('gh', ['api', 'graphql', '-f', `query=${query}`, ...Object.entries(vars).flatMap(([k, v]) => ['-F', `${k}=${v}`])]).toString()).data\nconst team = gql(`query($owner:String!,$team:String!){ organization(login:$owner){ team(slug:$team){ reviewRequestDelegationEnabled reviewRequestDelegationAlgorithm } } }`,\n  { owner, team: process.env.TEAM! }).organization.team\nconst prs = gql(`query($owner:String!,$name:String!){ repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){\n  pullRequests(first:100, states:MERGED, orderBy:{field:UPDATED_AT,direction:DESC}){ nodes{ number createdAt mergedAt author{login}\n    timelineItems(itemTypes:[READY_FOR_REVIEW_EVENT,REVIEW_REQUESTED_EVENT,PULL_REQUEST_REVIEW], first:50){ nodes{ __typename\n      ... on ReadyForReviewEvent{ createdAt } ... on ReviewRequestedEvent{ createdAt } ... on PullRequestReview{ author{login} } } } } } } }`,\n  { owner, name }).repository.pullRequests.nodes.filter((pr: any) => Date.parse(pr.mergedAt) > Date.now() - 30 * 86_400_000)\nconst perReviewer: Record<string, number> = {}\nlet slow = 0\nfor (const pr of prs) {\n  const items = pr.timelineItems.nodes\n  const ready = items.filter((i: any) => i.__typename === 'ReadyForReviewEvent').map((i: any) => i.createdAt).sort().pop() ?? pr.createdAt\n  const requested = items.find((i: any) => i.__typename === 'ReviewRequestedEvent' && i.createdAt >= ready)\n  if (!requested || Date.parse(requested.createdAt) - Date.parse(ready) > 15 * 60_000) slow++\n  for (const r of items.filter((i: any) => i.__typename === 'PullRequestReview' && i.author?.login !== pr.author?.login)) perReviewer[r.author.login] = (perReviewer[r.author.login] ?? 0) + 1\n}\nconst total = Object.values(perReviewer).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)\nconst top = Object.entries(perReviewer).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])[0]\nconsole.log({ prs: prs.length, slowRequests: slow, top, algorithm: team.reviewRequestDelegationAlgorithm })\nif (!team.reviewRequestDelegationEnabled || team.reviewRequestDelegationAlgorithm !== 'ROUND_ROBIN') { console.error('review assignment is not round robin'); process.exit(1) }\nif (slow > 0 || (top && top[1] > 0.4 * total)) process.exit(1)\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "OWNERSHIP": [
          "collective"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pr-size",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Pull requests stay small",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "A required check on every pull request sums additions and deletions over its files from the GitHub API (all pages, slurped into one array, since a large pull request has more than one page of files), skipping lock files, snapshots and generated code, and fails above the limit with the count; a lead may set the size-exception label, which the check honours.\n\nThreshold: Changed lines per pull request (additions + deletions, generated and lock files excluded) at most {{MAX_PR_LINES}}; the check fails above it unless the size-exception label is set\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api --paginate --slurp) as a required GitHub Actions check\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# required check \"pr-size\": fails above the limit of changed lines of source\nset -euo pipefail\nMAX={{MAX_PR_LINES}}\npr=$(jq -r .pull_request.number \"$GITHUB_EVENT_PATH\")\nlines=$(gh api --paginate --slurp \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/pulls/$pr/files\" \\\n  | jq '[.[][] | select(.filename | test(\"(package-lock\\\\.json|yarn\\\\.lock|pnpm-lock\\\\.yaml|\\\\.snap$|/generated/|\\\\.min\\\\.js$)\") | not)\n         | .additions + .deletions] | add // 0')\nlabels=$(gh pr view \"$pr\" --json labels -q '.labels[].name')\necho \"changed lines of source: $lines (limit $MAX)\"\nif [ \"$lines\" -gt \"$MAX\" ] && ! grep -qx size-exception <<< \"$labels\"; then\n  echo \"::error::$lines changed lines; split the pull request or ask a lead for the size-exception label\"; exit 1\nfi\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SIZE": [
          "limit"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pr-size-report",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Size shown on the pull request",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "A step on every pull request sums additions and deletions over its files from the GitHub API (all pages), skipping lock files, snapshots and generated code, and writes the count to the job summary as a notice; nothing blocks.\n\nThreshold: Every pull request's check summary shows its changed lines of source (additions + deletions, generated and lock files excluded); the step exits 0\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api --paginate --slurp) in a GitHub Actions step\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# pull-request step \"pr-size\": the count in the summary, never blocking\nset -euo pipefail\npr=$(jq -r .pull_request.number \"$GITHUB_EVENT_PATH\")\nlines=$(gh api --paginate --slurp \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/pulls/$pr/files\" \\\n  | jq '[.[][] | select(.filename | test(\"(package-lock\\\\.json|yarn\\\\.lock|pnpm-lock\\\\.yaml|\\\\.snap$|/generated/|\\\\.min\\\\.js$)\") | not)\n         | .additions + .deletions] | add // 0')\necho \"::notice::$lines changed lines of source\"\necho \"Changed lines of source: **$lines**\" >> \"$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SIZE": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-branch-age",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No long-lived branches",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "A daily job lists every branch, computes the age of its first commit not on main, and fails naming the branches over the limit and their last authors; Renovate and Dependabot branches are listed separately so a stale dependency update does not hide a stale feature branch, and the other way round.\n\nThreshold: 0 branches older than {{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}} days (first commit not on main) other than main, develop, release/* and hotfix/*; bot branches (renovate/, dependabot/) reported apart and not counted\n\nTool: git in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# daily: flags branches whose first own commit is older than the limit\nset -euo pipefail\nMAX={{MAX_BRANCH_DAYS}}\ngit fetch --prune origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*'\nnow=$(date +%s); stale=0\nfor b in $(git for-each-ref --format='%(refname:short)' refs/remotes/origin | grep -Ev '/(main|develop|HEAD)$|/(release|hotfix)/'); do\n  first=$(git log --format=%ct origin/main..\"$b\" | tail -1); [ -z \"$first\" ] && continue   # oldest commit not on main\n  age=$(( (now - first) / 86400 )); author=$(git log -1 --format=%an \"$b\")\n  case \"$b\" in\n    origin/renovate/*|origin/dependabot/*) echo \"bot: $b $age d\";;\n    *) if [ \"$age\" -gt \"$MAX\" ]; then echo \"::error::$b is $age days old (last: $author); merge, split or delete it\"; stale=$((stale+1)); fi;;\n  esac\ndone\n[ \"$stale\" -eq 0 ]\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-commitlint",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Commit messages are conventional",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "commitlint with the conventional config runs on every commit through a husky commit-msg hook, and again in CI over all commits of the pull request, so a commit made with --no-verify is still caught before the merge; a second job checks the pull request title, which becomes the subject on main under squash merges. semantic-release or release-please derives the changelog and version from the types.\n\nThreshold: 100% of commits in a pull request pass commitlint with the conventional config and a header of at most 72 characters; the pull request title passes the same check; the hook runs on every clone after npm install\n\nTool: commitlint with husky, and action-semantic-pull-request for the title\n\n```js\n// commitlint.config.js\nmodule.exports = {\n  extends: ['@commitlint/config-conventional'],\n  rules: {\n    'header-max-length': [2, 'always', 72],\n    'scope-enum': [2, 'always', ['api', 'web', 'payments', 'auth', 'db', 'ci', 'deps']],\n  },\n}\n// package.json: \"scripts\": { \"prepare\": \"husky\" }\n// .husky/commit-msg:   npx --no -- commitlint --edit \"$1\"\n// CI (pull_request):   npx commitlint --from \"$BASE_SHA\" --to \"$HEAD_SHA\" --verbose\n// the title, which squash merges put on main (.github/workflows/pr-title.yml, on: pull_request_target: types [opened, edited, synchronize]):\n//   - uses: amannn/action-semantic-pull-request@v6.1.1   # pin by commit SHA in your repository\n//     env: { GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }} }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "COMMITS": [
          "conventional"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-squash-subjects",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Squash merges with short subjects",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "A scheduled job reads the repository's merge settings through the API and fails unless squash is the only merge method, then walks the last 30 days of main and fails on a subject over 72 characters or one that does not end with a pull request number.\n\nThreshold: Repository allows squash merge only (merge commits and rebase merges off); 0 commits on main in the last 30 days with a subject over 72 characters or without a pull request number\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api) with git in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY\" | jq -e '.allow_squash_merge and (.allow_merge_commit|not) and (.allow_rebase_merge|not)' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"::error::squash must be the only merge method\"; exit 1; }\ngit fetch --no-tags origin main\nbad=0\nwhile IFS= read -r subject; do\n  if [ \"${#subject}\" -gt 72 ] || ! grep -Eq '\\(#[0-9]+\\)$' <<< \"$subject\"; then echo \"::error::bad subject on main: $subject\"; bad=$((bad+1)); fi\ndone < <(git log --since='30 days ago' --no-merges --format=%s origin/main)\n[ \"$bad\" -eq 0 ]\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "COMMITS": [
          "free"
        ],
        "BRANCHING": [
          "trunk",
          "feature-pr"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-develop-subjects",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Squash merges into develop with short subjects",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "Under GitFlow the release and hotfix merges into main are merge commits, so the squash rule applies to develop: a scheduled job checks that rebase merging is off (squash for feature pull requests, merge commits for releases), then walks the non-merge commits of develop in the last 30 days and fails on a subject over 72 characters or without a pull request number.\n\nThreshold: Repository allows squash and merge commits, rebase merges off; 0 non-merge commits on develop in the last 30 days with a subject over 72 characters or without a pull request number\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh api) with git in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY\" | jq -e '.allow_squash_merge and .allow_merge_commit and (.allow_rebase_merge|not)' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"::error::squash (features) and merge commits (releases) on, rebase merges off\"; exit 1; }\ngit fetch --no-tags origin develop\nbad=0\nwhile IFS= read -r subject; do\n  if [ \"${#subject}\" -gt 72 ] || ! grep -Eq '\\(#[0-9]+\\)$' <<< \"$subject\"; then echo \"::error::bad subject on develop: $subject\"; bad=$((bad+1)); fi\ndone < <(git log --since='30 days ago' --no-merges --first-parent --format=%s origin/develop)\n[ \"$bad\" -eq 0 ]\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "COMMITS": [
          "free"
        ],
        "BRANCHING": [
          "gitflow"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-review-turnaround",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Reviews come within hours",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "holistic",
      "description": "A weekly job reads the last 100 merged pull requests through the GraphQL API, keeps the ones merged in the last 30 days, takes the later of each one's creation and its last ready-for-review event as the start and the first review (which also covers review comments) or conversation comment by someone other than the author as the end, counts working hours (Mon–Fri, 9–17 in the runner's TZ) between them, and fails on the percentiles; the numbers go to the team's dashboard beside the DORA lead time.\n\nThreshold: Over the pull requests merged in the last 30 days, working hours from ready-for-review to the first review, review comment or comment by someone else: median under {{REVIEW_HOURS}}, 90th percentile under 8\n\nTool: GitHub GraphQL API (gh api graphql) with a Node script\n\n```ts\n// scripts/review-turnaround.ts  (weekly; TZ=Europe/Warsaw)\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nconst REVIEW_HOURS = {{REVIEW_HOURS}}\nconst q = `query($owner:String!,$name:String!){ repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){\n  pullRequests(first:100, states:MERGED, orderBy:{field:UPDATED_AT,direction:DESC}){ nodes{ number createdAt mergedAt author{login}\n    timelineItems(itemTypes:[READY_FOR_REVIEW_EVENT,PULL_REQUEST_REVIEW,ISSUE_COMMENT], first:50){ nodes{ __typename\n      ... on ReadyForReviewEvent{ createdAt } ... on PullRequestReview{ createdAt author{login} } ... on IssueComment{ createdAt author{login} } } } } } } }`\nconst [owner, name] = process.env.GITHUB_REPOSITORY!.split('/')\nconst data = JSON.parse(execFileSync('gh', ['api', 'graphql', '-f', `query=${q}`, '-F', `owner=${owner}`, '-F', `name=${name}`]).toString())\nconst workingHours = (from: Date, to: Date): number => {   // Mon–Fri 9–17, hour by hour\n  let h = 0\n  for (const t = new Date(from); t < to; t.setHours(t.getHours() + 1)) if (t.getDay() >= 1 && t.getDay() <= 5 && t.getHours() >= 9 && t.getHours() < 17) h++\n  return h\n}\nconst hours = data.data.repository.pullRequests.nodes\n  .filter((pr: any) => Date.parse(pr.mergedAt) > Date.now() - 30 * 86_400_000)\n  .flatMap((pr: any) => {\n    const items = pr.timelineItems.nodes\n    const ready = items.filter((i: any) => i.__typename === 'ReadyForReviewEvent').map((i: any) => i.createdAt).sort().pop() ?? pr.createdAt\n    const first = items.find((i: any) => i.__typename !== 'ReadyForReviewEvent' && i.author?.login !== pr.author?.login && i.createdAt > ready)\n    return first ? [workingHours(new Date(ready), new Date(first.createdAt))] : []\n  }).sort((a: number, b: number) => a - b)\nconst p = (n: number) => hours[Math.floor(hours.length * n)] ?? 0\nconsole.log({ prs: hours.length, p50: p(0.5), p90: p(0.9) })\nif (p(0.5) > REVIEW_HOURS || p(0.9) > 8) process.exit(1)\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-critical-two-approvals",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Critical paths get two approvals",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "GitHub rulesets cannot vary the approval count by path, so a required check does it: on every review and push it lists the changed files, and when one is under a critical path it counts the latest review per reviewer (so a reviewer who later requested changes no longer counts) by people other than the author and fails below two. With CODEOWNERS, the ruleset requires the owning team's approval on top.\n\nThreshold: A pull request touching {{CRITICAL_PATHS}} merges only with 2 current approvals from non-authors; 0 such merges with fewer\n\nTool: GitHub Actions with gh (required check \"critical-paths-review\")\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/critical-paths-review.yml\nname: critical-paths-review\non:\n  pull_request: { types: [opened, synchronize, ready_for_review] }\n  pull_request_review: { types: [submitted, dismissed] }\njobs:\n  check:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    env:\n      GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}\n      PR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}\n      CRITICAL: '{{CRITICAL_PATHS}}'\n    steps:\n      - run: |\n          pattern=\"^($(sed -E 's#/##g; s/, */|/g' <<< \"$CRITICAL\"))/\"      # \"payments/, auth/, migrations/\" -> ^(payments|auth|migrations)/\n          files=$(gh api --paginate \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/pulls/$PR/files\" -q '.[].filename')\n          grep -Eq \"$pattern\" <<< \"$files\" || { echo \"no critical path touched\"; exit 0; }\n          author=$(gh pr view \"$PR\" --json author -q .author.login)\n          approvals=$(gh pr view \"$PR\" --json latestReviews -q \"[.latestReviews[] | select(.state==\\\"APPROVED\\\" and .author.login!=\\\"$author\\\") | .author.login] | unique | length\")\n          [ \"$approvals\" -ge 2 ] || { echo \"::error::critical path changed; $approvals of 2 approvals\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REVIEW": [
          "two-critical"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pair-trailer",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Pair changes name their partner",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1560,
      "category": "process",
      "description": "A required check reads the pull request's commits and its latest review per reviewer: when every commit carries a Co-authored-by trailer, an approval from anyone but the author is enough (the partner's included); otherwise the approver must have written none of the commits, so each approver's login is resolved to e-mail addresses through the users API (the public e-mail and the two noreply forms) and compared with the trailers. Pairs set the trailer once with git-mob or a commit template.\n\nThreshold: A pull request with a Co-authored-by trailer on 100% of its commits merges on any current approval by a non-author; one without merges only on an approval from someone whose e-mail is neither the author's nor in any trailer\n\nTool: GitHub Actions with gh and git interpret-trailers (required check \"pair-or-review\")\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# required check \"pair-or-review\": a pair's partner may approve; a solo change needs an outsider\nset -euo pipefail\nauthor=$(gh pr view \"$PR\" --json author -q .author.login)\napprovers=$(gh pr view \"$PR\" --json latestReviews -q '.latestReviews[] | select(.state==\"APPROVED\") | .author.login' | sort -u | grep -vx \"$author\" || true)\n[ -n \"$approvers\" ] || { echo \"::error::no approval from anyone but the author\"; exit 1; }\ntrailers=\"\"; solo=0\nfor sha in $(gh pr view \"$PR\" --json commits -q '.commits[].oid'); do\n  t=$(git log -1 --format=%B \"$sha\" | git interpret-trailers --parse | grep -i '^Co-authored-by:' || true)\n  [ -n \"$t\" ] || solo=1\n  trailers=\"$trailers $(tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' <<< \"$t\")\"\ndone\nif [ \"$solo\" -eq 0 ]; then echo \"pair change: Co-authored-by on every commit, the partner's approval counts\"; exit 0; fi\nemails_of() { gh api \"users/$1\" --jq '[.email // empty, \"\\(.id)+\\(.login)@users.noreply.github.com\", \"\\(.login)@users.noreply.github.com\"] | .[]' | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'; }\nfor a in $approvers; do   # a solo change: the approver must not be one of its writers\n  outsider=1\n  for e in $(emails_of \"$a\"); do grep -qF \"<$e>\" <<< \"$trailers\" && outsider=0; done\n  [ \"$outsider\" -eq 1 ] && { echo \"approved by $a, who wrote none of the commits\"; exit 0; }\ndone\necho \"::error::not every commit names a partner, and every approver co-wrote the change; ask someone else to review\"; exit 1\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REVIEW": [
          "pairing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-gitflow-history",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Main holds only releases, develop is protected",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1690,
      "category": "structural",
      "description": "A job on every push to main checks that each merged branch was a release or hotfix branch, that the merge is tagged with a semantic version, and that the tagged commit is reachable from develop (the back-merge happened); it fails otherwise, so a forgotten back-merge is found the same day and not in the next release. It also reads the rules on develop and fails when develop takes direct or force pushes.\n\nThreshold: 100% of merge commits on main come from release/* or hotfix/*; every merge to main carries an annotated tag vX.Y.Z; 0 tags on main absent from develop; the rules on develop include pull_request and non_fast_forward\n\nTool: git and gh api in a GitHub Actions job on push to main\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# on push to main: release and hotfix merges only, tagged and back-merged; develop protected\nset -euo pipefail\ngit fetch origin develop --tags\nfor m in $(git log --merges --first-parent --format=%H origin/main~20..origin/main); do\n  src=$(git log -1 --format=%s \"$m\" | grep -oE \"(release|hotfix)/[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\" || true)\n  [ -n \"$src\" ] || { echo \"::error::$m merged into main from something other than release/* or hotfix/*\"; exit 1; }\n  tag=$(git tag --points-at \"$m\" | grep -E '^v[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$' || true)\n  [ -n \"$tag\" ] || { echo \"::error::merge $m of $src has no vX.Y.Z tag\"; exit 1; }\n  git merge-base --is-ancestor \"$m\" origin/develop || { echo \"::error::$tag not merged back into develop\"; exit 1; }\ndone\ngh api \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/rules/branches/develop\" | jq -e '(map(select(.type==\"pull_request\")) | length>0) and (map(select(.type==\"non_fast_forward\")) | length>0)' > /dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"::error::develop is not protected (pull request required, no force push)\"; exit 1; }\necho \"main holds tagged releases, all back-merged; develop is protected\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "gitflow"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-daily-integration",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Everyone integrates daily",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1820,
      "category": "holistic",
      "description": "A weekly job groups the merges to main by author and day and compares them with the days each person committed anywhere; a developer committing for days without merging is working on a hidden branch, which is what trunk-based development is meant to prevent. The branch age itself is the daily branch report's job. The result sits beside the DORA lead time on the team's dashboard.\n\nThreshold: Over the last 14 days, every developer with a commit merged to main on at least 80% of the days they committed\n\nTool: git in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# weekly: days committed vs. days merged to main, per author, last 14 days\nset -euo pipefail\ngit fetch origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*'\nsince='14 days ago'\ncommitted=$(git log --all --since=\"$since\" --format='%ae %as' | sort -u)\nmerged=$(git log origin/main --since=\"$since\" --format='%ae %as' | sort -u)\nfail=0\nfor a in $(cut -d' ' -f1 <<< \"$committed\" | sort -u); do\n  c=$(grep -c \"^$a \" <<< \"$committed\"); m=$(grep -c \"^$a \" <<< \"$merged\" || true)\n  echo \"$a: committed on $c days, merged to main on $m\"\n  [ $((m * 10)) -ge $((c * 8)) ] || { echo \"::error::$a merged on fewer than 80% of their active days\"; fail=1; }\ndone\nexit $fail\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BRANCHING": [
          "trunk"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
